   "heading": "Ups and downs notation",
   "level": 3,
   "text": "81edo can be notated with ups and downs , spoken as up, dup, dudsharp, downsharp, sharp, upsharp etc. and down, dud, dupflat etc. Note that dudsharp is equivalent to trup (triple-up) and dupflat is equivalent to trud (triple-down).\nAnother notation uses alternative ups and downs . Here, this can be done using sharps and flats with arrows, borrowed from extended Helmholtz–Ellis notation :"
  },
  {
   "heading": "Tom Winspear's notation",
   "level": 3,
   "text": "81edo accidentals created and used by Tom Winspear , based on those provided in Scala though with a logic correction. The innermost accidentals represent one edostep, followed by two, then the bracket representing three. What appears to be a half-sharp is actually a fifth-sharp. Conventional sharp/doublesharp/flat/doubleflat accidentals are reached in steps of five and the pattern repeats itself on them. The chromatic scale can be notated utilizing only six accidentals in either direction—the rest are for enharmonics."
  },
